 "section_summary": "This section covers the aftermath and political consequences of the February 26, 1936 Incident in Tokyo through U.S. Embassy despatches, State Department internal memoranda, a diplomatic telegram, and a detailed contemporary newspaper clipping. It documents the court-martial verdicts, sentences, and executions of the military and civilian conspirators, the official statement detailing their motives and assassination plans, and the termination of martial law in Tokyo on July 17. The documentation also analyzes the Hirota Cabinet's difficulties in coordinating ministerial reform proposals, the Army's dominance under General Terauchi in securing immense defense budgets at the expense of social welfare programs, and the broader trends toward state economic control and zaibatsu accommodation.",
 "subfiles": [
  {
   "about": "Reports on proposed ministerial reform policies under consideration by the Hirota Cabinet, post-incident foreign policy directions, and the reaction of major zaibatsu houses to the February 26 Incident.",
   "continues_previous": true,
   "date": "1936-07-08",
   "doc_type": "despatch",
   "end_page": 239,
   "from": "Joseph C. Grew",
   "keywords": [
    "February 26 Incident",
    "Hirota Cabinet",
    "national defense replenishment",
    "taxation reform",
    "electric power nationalization",
    "fuel policy",
    "zaibatsu",
    "Mitsui",
    "administrative reform"
   ],
   "start_page": 231,
   "summary": "Continuing from an earlier page, this despatch outlines six key national policy proposals submitted to the Cabinet by government ministries, including defense replenishment, tax reform, electric power nationalization, central fuel control, extension of compulsory education, and agricultural land reform. It discusses Prime Minister Hirota's administrative reform dilemmas, including Army proposals for a Ministry of Air and a Ministry of Health. It also describes Japan's independent continental foreign policy towards China and the Soviet Union, as well as measures taken by Mitsui, Mitsubishi, and Sumitomo to conciliate public and military sentiment by retiring senior leaders, floating subsidiary shares, and funding defense-related industries.",
   "title": "Tokyo Despatch No. 1935: Political Developments and Proposed Cabinet Reforms Following the February 26 Incident",
   "to": "Secretary of State"
  },
  {
   "about": "A newspaper report detailing the court-martial death sentences and prison terms imposed on the February 26 Incident conspirators and publishing the full War Office statement on the rebellion.",
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1936-07-08",
   "doc_type": "clipping",
   "end_page": 246,
   "from": "The Japan Advertiser",
   "keywords": [
    "February 26 Incident",
    "Tokyo Court Martial",
    "death sentences",
    "War Office statement",
    "Showa Restoration",
    "Terujiro Kita",
    "Saburo Aizawa",
    "Korekiyo Takahashi",
    "Jotaro Watanabe",
    "Makoto Saito"
   ],
   "start_page": 240,
   "summary": "The Japan Advertiser reports that seventeen leaders of the February 26 uprising (thirteen former military officers and four civilians) were sentenced to death by the Tokyo Court Martial, with fifty-nine others receiving prison terms ranging from two years to life, and forty-seven acquitted. It reproduces the comprehensive War Office statement detailing the participants' ideological motives, their connections to ultranationalist thinkers Terujiro Kita and Mitsugu Nishida, and their invocation of the Showa Restoration. The article recounts in chronological detail the planning, the armed assaults against high officials including Premier Okada, Finance Minister Takahashi, Viscount Saito, General Watanabe, Admiral Suzuki, and Count Makino, the seizure of government buildings, and the final surrender.",
   "title": "17 Incident Rebels to Die; Details Revealed of Plot and Manner of Execution (The Japan Advertiser)",
   "to": ""
  },
  {
