   "start_page": 251,
   "summary": "Despatch No. 1952 reports the July 12 execution of fifteen participants in the February 26 uprising (thirteen former army officers and two civilians) with little public outcry, and the lifting of martial law in Tokyo on July 17. It details the abandonment of general Cabinet discussions on over thirty national reform measures due to internal divisions and military insistence on budgetary priority. The despatch analyzes the Army's enormous twelve-year replenishment program under General Terauchi, secret naval expansion plans, proposed tax increases, and proposed national mobilization legislation, concluding that the military has asserted firm control over government policy while public debate has helped educate the populace on complex state issues.",
   "title": "Tokyo Despatch No. 1952: Aftermath of the February 26 Incident",
   "to": "Secretary of State"
  },
  {
   "about": "A State Department memorandum summarizing Tokyo Despatch No. 1955 on the political consequences and strengthening of military influence following the February 26 Incident.",
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1936-08-20",
   "doc_type": "memorandum",
   "end_page": 262,
   "from": "Eugene H. Dooman",
   "keywords": [
