    "state control",
    "capital flight"
   ],
   "start_page": 260,
   "summary": "Dooman on Despatch No. 1955: the public refused to back the February 26 insurgents; financial anxiety followed. The Army emerged stronger, adopting the rebels' anti-privilege and state control demands.",
   "title": "Memorandum on Tokyo Despatch No. 1955 Regarding the Political Consequences of the February 26 Incident",
   "to": "Stanley K. Hornbeck"
  },
  {
   "about": "A comprehensive analysis of the political consequences of the February 26 Incident, examining public reaction, army leverage over the Hirota Cabinet, financial repercussions, and the push for fundamental reform.",
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1936-07-23",
   "doc_type": "despatch",
   "end_page": 268,
   "from": "Joseph C. Grew",
   "keywords": [
    "February 26 Incident",
    "political consequences",
    "Hirota Cabinet",
    "army leadership",
    "Shigeru Yoshida",
    "Saburo Aizawa",
    "Jinzaburo Mazaki",
    "state control",
    "Showa Restoration"
   ],
   "start_page": 263,
   "summary": "Ambassador Grew provides a detailed study of the political aftermath of the February 26 uprising, recounting the rebellion, martial law, court-martial proceedings, executions, and the lifting of martial law. He analyzes why the insurgents failed to win popular backing, citing public inertia and moral disgust at political assassinations, while pointing out that the Army leveraged the crisis to impose its reform agenda on Premier Hirota and veto cabinet choices such as Yoshida. The despatch traces financial tremors including bank deposit declines and capital flight controls, reviews the trial and execution of Lieutenant-Colonel Aizawa, and concludes that the Army has successfully positioned itself as the indispensable trustee of fundamental political and economic reform.",
   "title": "Tokyo Despatch No. 1955: The Political Consequences of the Tokyo Incident of February 26, 1936",
   "to": "Secretary of State"
  }
